AbraFlexi-Revolut for Debian
=============================

PHP Autoloader
--------------

This package ships a hand-written Debian-native autoloader (not Composer's
vendor/autoload.php, and not phpab-generated).

Classes are installed in: `/usr/lib/abraflexi-revolut/`
Autoloader: `/usr/share/php/abraflexi-revolut/autoload.php`

To use in your PHP code:

```php
require_once '/usr/share/php/abraflexi-revolut/autoload.php';
```

The autoloader (`debian/autoload.php` in the source tree) registers a PSR-4
loader for the `Vitexsoftware\AbraflexiRevolut\` namespace against
`/usr/lib/abraflexi-revolut/`, and also requires the Debian-packaged
`AbraFlexi` and `Ease` library autoloaders it depends on.

**Note:** This package does NOT ship vendor/autoload.php from Composer.
Use the Debian autoloader above instead for proper system integration.

Installation
------------

For Debian, Ubuntu & friends please use the VitexSoftware repository:

```shell
sudo apt install lsb-release wget apt-transport-https bzip2
wget -qO- https://repo.vitexsoftware.com/KEY.gpg | sudo tee /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/vitexsoftware.gpg
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/vitexsoftware.gpg] https://repo.vitexsoftware.com $(lsb_release -sc) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/vitexsoftware.list
sudo apt update
sudo apt install abraflexi-revolut
```

Related packages built from this same source:

- `multiflexi-abraflexi-revolut` — registers this app in a
  [MultiFlexi](https://multiflexi.eu) installation.
- `abraflexi-revolut-statement-downloader` — browser-automated Revolut
  statement download (QR-code login), independent of the PHP importer;
  see `/usr/share/doc/abraflexi-revolut-statement-downloader/README.md`
  after installing it.

Migration from Composer vendor/autoload.php
--------------------------------------------

If you're migrating code that currently does:

```php
require_once __DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php';
```

replace it with:

```php
require_once '/usr/share/php/abraflexi-revolut/autoload.php';
```

This ensures you're using the system-wide Debian packages instead of a
bundled `vendor/` directory.

